---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Term from "../components/Term.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "../site.config.mjs";

const KILL = `<i># docs/PLAN.md</i>
If the org arm does not beat the single-agent arm on the
pre-registered rubric — blind-judged quality, verified external
evidence, cost, wall-clock — then yaaf-as-an-org-framework
is <b>shelved</b>.`;

const ROWS = [
  ["Shape", "One assistant", "One agent", "A library you wire up", "An org"],
  ["Weight", "A daemon", "A daemon", "An import, nothing to deploy", "Postgres and two containers"],
  ["Optimises for", "Reach", "Continuity", "Composition", "Accountability"],
  ["Time horizon", "A session", "An ongoing chat", "A run", "Weeks"],
  ["Who picks the next task", "You", "You", "Your code", "A CEO"],
  ["“Done” means", "You say so", "You say so", "The graph returned", "The platform verified it"],
  ["Cost control", "Your own instrumentation", "Your own instrumentation", "Your own instrumentation", "Capped, pauses itself"],
  ["Disagreement", "—", "—", "Whatever you author", "Roles that can veto"],
  ["The record", "Chat history", "Its memory", "Traces, if you wired them", "Every message, run, dollar, decision, refusal"],
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Why yaaf"
  description="How yaaf differs from agent frameworks like LangGraph and CrewAI, from personal agents like OpenClaw and Hermes, and from a good skill. A framework composes agents. yaaf governs them."
>
  <Section
    eyebrow="Different how?"
    title="Three questions get asked every time."
    lede="Fair answers, because the honest comparison is more persuasive than a flattering one, and because two of the three are not competitors at all."
  >
    <ol class="qs">
      <li><a href="#frameworks">How is this different from an agent framework?</a></li>
      <li><a href="#assistants">How is this different from OpenClaw or Hermes?</a></li>
      <li><a href="#skill">Isn't this just a skill?</a></li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <Section eyebrow="Question one" title="A framework composes agents. yaaf governs them." id="frameworks" grid={true}
    lede="LangGraph, CrewAI, AutoGen and friends solve a real problem: getting several models to pass work between each other. That problem is upstream of the one yaaf is about.">
    <div class="two">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="eyebrow">A framework gives you</p>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li>A way to wire agents into a graph</li>
          <li>Coordination you write as code</li>
          <li>Rules expressed as prompts</li>
          <li>“Done” when the graph returns</li>
          <li>Whatever it cost, after it cost it</li>
          <li>Traces, if you instrumented them</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="col hi">
        <p class="eyebrow">yaaf adds the part underneath</p>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li>Agents act only through an authenticated API, on short-lived per-run tokens</li>
          <li>Every rule is an HTTP refusal or an automatic state change, never a sentence in a prompt</li>
          <li>Work that makes nothing true is refused: every task must serve a requirement that carries its own test</li>
          <li>Only the platform can write “met”. There is no agent verb for it</li>
          <li>A budget that warns at 80% and pauses the project at the cap, killed runs included</li>
          <li>A delivery date the platform stamps from measured pace and re-projects every tick</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="quote">
      <p>A prompt is a suggestion, and a clever model talks itself around one. So the rules live in the server, not the prompt. An agent can't be argued out of a rule it never held — break one and it doesn't get a stern reminder, it gets a 403.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="after">
      Use a framework when you are building the flow. Reach for yaaf when the flow
      has to survive six weeks without you and be defensible afterwards to a client,
      an auditor, or yourself. They are not the same tool, and yaaf is the slower,
      heavier one on purpose.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <Section eyebrow="Question two" title="OpenClaw and Hermes make one agent better. yaaf makes many agents accountable." id="assistants"
    lede="Both are excellent, both are MIT, both are self-hosted, and neither is trying to do what yaaf is trying to do. The axes are different.">
    <div class="three">
      <article class="who">
        <p class="eyebrow">OpenClaw · MIT</p>
        <h3>Reach</h3>
        <p class="tl">“The AI that really does things.”</p>
        <p>A local-first assistant that meets you in the channels you already use: Discord, Slack, iMessage, Telegram, WhatsApp and twenty more — on any OS. One gateway, one assistant, everywhere you are.</p>
        <a href="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw" rel="noopener">github.com/openclaw/openclaw ↗</a>
      </article>
      <article class="who">
        <p class="eyebrow">Hermes Agent · MIT</p>
        <h3>Continuity</h3>
        <p class="tl">“The agent that grows with you.”</p>
        <p>One self-hosted agent with persistent memory and a closed learning loop: after a hard task it writes itself a reusable skill, so it is more capable the longer you use it.</p>
        <a href="https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent" rel="noopener">github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent ↗</a>
      </article>
      <article class="who me">
        <p class="eyebrow">yaaf · Apache-2.0</p>
        <h3>Accountability</h3>
        <p class="tl">“Stop prompting, start delegating.”</p>
        <p>An org: roles with their own charters, a CEO that picks the next piece of work, a budget that stops itself, a date the platform owns, and a record of every dollar and every refusal.</p>
        <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener">the repository ↗</a>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="mid">
      The compressed version: <strong>they are one agent, in a conversation, with you
      present.</strong> yaaf is <strong>many agents, accountable to each other, over
      weeks, with you absent.</strong>
    </p>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>OpenClaw</th><th>Hermes Agent</th><th>Orchestration libraries</th><th class="me">yaaf</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {ROWS.map(([label, a, b, c, d]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{label}</th>
              <td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td><td>{c}</td><td class="me">{d}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="tnote">
      Written by yaaf, so read the rows as claims rather than findings, and the
      second one is the one to hold against it. LangGraph, CrewAI and AutoGen are
      libraries you import; yaaf is a server you run. On a task that fits in one
      sitting that trade is simply bad, and no row below buys it back.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p class="eyebrow">And the part that matters most</p>
      <p>
        <strong>These are not competitors.</strong> yaaf is an MCP server with thirty-one
        tools across four scopes. Anything that speaks MCP is a front end for it,
        including a personal assistant. Chair a yaaf org from wherever you already
        talk to an agent; yaaf is the floor underneath, not the window.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <Section eyebrow="Question three" title="Isn't this just a skill?" id="skill" grid={true}
    lede="yaaf runs your skills: a role's skill list is an allowlist handed straight to the Agent SDK. A skill is a capability. yaaf decides which capability to point at what, checks it got done, pays for it from a capped budget, and keeps the receipt.">
    <div class="two">
      <div class="col">
        <p class="eyebrow">Use a skill when</p>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li>You're watching</li>
          <li>It finishes in one sitting</li>
          <li>Nothing irreversible happens</li>
          <li>Nobody asks in six weeks how you know</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="col hi">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reach for yaaf when</p>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li>It has to run unattended for weeks</li>
          <li>“Done” can't be self-graded</li>
          <li>Money has to stop by itself</li>
          <li>You'll have to reconstruct it later for a client or an auditor</li>
          <li>You want agents to disagree, because one agent checking its own work has the same blind spots in both seats</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="after">
      A good model with a good skill out-writes yaaf's agents on any single task.{" "}
      <strong>The difference isn't capability. It's governance and duration.</strong>
    </p>
  </Section>

  <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <Section eyebrow="The honest part" title="Nobody has proven the org beats one good agent.">
    <div class="honest">
      <p>
        And yaaf doesn't claim to have. Hierarchy's main product is lossy
        summarisation at each boundary; the CEO's judgement (what to queue, kill
        and prioritise) is the actual bet, and everything else is transport. That
        question is open, and nothing in the repository settles it.
      </p>
      <p>
        What the repository does give you is a fair way to settle it yourself.{" "}
        <strong>Solo mode</strong> runs a single agent under byte-identical
        governance, so you can hand the same goal to a hierarchy and to one brain
        and compare the bills.
      </p>
      <Term label="The kill condition, in the repo, in public" code={KILL} />
      <p>
        The salvage path is already built and already usable: yaaf as a governance
        harness for a single agent: queues, requirements, verification, budgets,
        approvals and audit. <a href="/docs/plan/">The whole plan, including what is
        deliberately not built.</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <Section eyebrow="Colophon" title="Who's behind this" grid={true}>
    <div class="bio">
      <p>
        yaaf is one person's answer to a question that kept coming up while building
        startups: the thing that made a team work was never a smarter individual. It
        was the roles. Who owns what, who checks it, who is allowed to say no.
      </p>
      <p>
        The code was written fast, with Claude Code. The design wasn't. It is years
        of running teams, compiled, and then held honest by a test suite that
        attacks every rule over HTTP the way an agent would, with forged tokens,
        wrong roles and rephrased requests.
      </p>
      <p>
        And the part you would find in one click: the git history is <strong>two weeks
        old</strong> — restarted on 30 August, so the repository is younger than the
        project, and the screenshots on the home page are stamped a week before its
        first commit. Roughly half the commits are authored by Claude Code. What
        that bought is a policy engine with 1,347 tests attacking it over HTTP. What
        it has not bought is a month of someone else's use, a published bill, or a
        security review by anyone who did not write it. The <a href="/docs/plan/">plan</a>{" "}
        says which claims are load-bearing; <a href="/docs/security/">the threat model</a>{" "}
        says what contains an agent and what does not.
      </p>
      <p class="sig">
        Pre-1.0, actively developed, Apache-2.0. Issues and pull requests welcome.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .qs { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2em; display: grid; gap: 10px; }
  .qs li { color: var(--color-muted); font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .qs a { color: var(--color-ink); text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 50%, transparent); }
  .qs a:hover { color: var(--color-accent-hi); }

  .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 12px; }
  .col { background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: var(--radius-card); padding: 18px; }
  .col.hi { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 45%, transparent); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 8%, var(--color-surface)); }
  ul.plain { list-style: none; margin: 14px 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 9px; }
  ul.plain li { font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--color-ink-2); padding-left: 18px; position: relative; }
  ul.plain li::before { content: "▸"; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-muted); font-size: 10px; top: 4px; }
  .col.hi ul.plain li::before { color: var(--color-accent); }

  .quote {
    margin-top: 22px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent-2);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent-2) 8%, transparent);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-card) var(--radius-card) 0;
    padding: 16px 20px;
  }
  .quote p { margin: 0; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-ink); max-width: 64ch; }
  .after { margin: 22px 0 0; color: var(--color-ink-2); font-size: 15px; max-width: 64ch; line-height: 1.65; }

  .three { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 12px; }
  .who { background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: var(--radius-card); padding: 18px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
  .who h3 { margin: 6px 0 0; font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 640; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .who .tl { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; color: var(--color-muted); }
  .who p { margin: 0; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-ink-2); }
  .who a { margin-top: auto; padding-top: 10px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; color: var(--color-muted); text-decoration: none; }
  .who a:hover { color: var(--color-accent-hi); }
  .who.me { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 55%, transparent); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 10%, var(--color-surface)); }
  .who.me h3 { color: var(--color-accent-hi); }

  .mid { margin: 26px 0; font-size: 1.125rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-ink-2); max-width: 62ch; }
  .mid strong { color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 620; }

  .tnote { margin: 0 0 14px; font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-muted); max-width: 70ch; }
  .tablewrap { overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: var(--radius-card); }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 13.5px; min-width: 720px; }
  thead th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 1.1px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  thead th.me { color: var(--color-accent-hi); }
  tbody th {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    padding: 11px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line-2);
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: 12.5px;
  }
  tbody td { padding: 11px 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line-2); color: var(--color-ink-2); vertical-align: top; }
  tbody td.me { color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 560; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 7%, transparent); }
  tbody tr:last-child th, tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }

  .callout { margin-top: 22px; border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 45%, transparent); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 9%, transparent); border-radius: var(--radius-card); padding: 18px 20px; }
  .callout p:last-child { margin: 10px 0 0; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--color-ink-2); max-width: 66ch; }
  .callout strong { color: var(--color-ink); }

  .honest { max-width: 66ch; display: grid; gap: 16px; }
  .honest p { margin: 0; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2); }
  .honest strong { color: var(--color-ink); }
  .honest a { color: var(--color-accent-hi); }

  .bio { max-width: 62ch; display: grid; gap: 14px; }
  .bio p { margin: 0; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-ink-2); }
  .sig { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px !important; color: var(--color-muted) !important; letter-spacing: 0.4px; }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .two, .three { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
</style>
